{# Rendered via Base\Admin\Widget\AnalyticsCardWidgetType - 'series'/'change'/
   'palette'/'visibleSeries' arrive as template vars (getTemplateVars()),
   not fetched here, so this template stays agnostic of the data source and
   reusable across multiple analytics_card instances with different params
   (see MenuItem::block()'s $instanceKey/$params).

   One shared-axis chart, series togglable per instance (params.series) -
   human-scale traffic and bot/AI-scale traffic DO flatten each other on
   one linear axis when plotted together, but that's now something the
   admin curates by turning a series off (e.g. hide Human to actually see
   Bot/AI's shape) instead of something this template forces on every
   instance unconditionally.

   The title stays fully in-place (see data-analytics-title, made editable
   by layout.html.twig's setCustomizing() while body.is-customizing) - no
   settings-panel field duplicates that. Which series show is controlled
   by the chart's own legend alone now (Chart.js already toggles a series
   on a legend click for free; admin-charts.js's legendOnClick adds a DOM
   event on top so layout.html.twig can persist it into params.series
   while in customize mode - a plain click outside customize mode stays a
   harmless, un-persisted view preference). A separate explicit gear-icon
   settings panel duplicating the exact same checkboxes existed here
   before and was removed - it read as redundant once it was clear the
   legend already does the whole job, persistence included, while in
   customize mode. (An EARLIER iteration had gone the other direction -
   legend-only with no gear at all - and reverted specifically because
   admins expected an explicit settings entry point too; if this removal
   gets the same reaction, that's the fallback to revisit, not a dead
   end.)

   ALL 5 series are always sent to the chart (datasets never omit a
   series just because params.series doesn't currently include it) with
   Chart.js's own per-dataset `hidden` flag instead - an OMITTED series
   would have no legend entry to click back on, which is exactly how a
   hidden series gets re-shown.

   'embedded' (set by composite.html.twig when this renders as one pane of
   a CompositeWidgetType, unset/false at the top level) drops the .card
   class - the outer composite already supplies ONE shared border/
   background for every pane, so a pane keeping its own .card would nest
   a card inside a card, which this codebase's dashboard loop explicitly
   never allows ("nothing is ever a card nested inside another card").
   .analytics-card's own padding is kept either way. #}
{# Summed from the VISIBLE source lines, not from Analytics' combined
   `pageViews` column, so the headline number always equals the lines the
   legend below is currently showing. It used to read the combined column
   unconditionally, which counted crawlers into "Vues" even while the
   chart's own bot line was hidden. The legend handler in layout.html.twig
   keeps that invariant afterwards (recomputeStatsFromChart sums exactly
   the datasets that aren't hidden). #}
{# Absent when a caller renders this card without it (older callers,
   composite panes): no retention line rather than a Twig error. #}
{% set retention = retention|default({visitors: null, users: null}) %}
{% set totalPageViews = series|reduce((carry, day) => carry + visibleSources|reduce((sum, key) => sum + day[key], 0), 0) %}
<div class="analytics-card {{ (embedded is not defined or not embedded) ? 'card' }}" data-analytics-card>
    <div class="analytics-card-header">
        <h2 class="analytics-card-title" data-analytics-title>{{ widget.label|trans({}, 'admin') }}</h2>
        <div class="analytics-stat-row">
            <div class="analytics-stat" data-stat="pageViews">
                <span class="analytics-stat-value">
                    <span class="analytics-stat-number" data-raw-value="{{ totalPageViews }}">{{ totalPageViews }}</span>
                    {%- if pageViewsChange is not null %}
                        <span class="analytics-stat-change {{ pageViewsChange >= 0 ? 'is-up' : 'is-down' }}">
                            {{ pageViewsChange >= 0 ? '+' : '' }}{{ pageViewsChange }}%
                        </span>
                    {% endif %}
                </span>
                <span class="analytics-stat-label">{{ 'analytics.label.page_views'|trans({}, 'admin') }}</span>
            </div>
            {% for key in ['uniqueVisitors', 'uniqueUsers', 'uniqueViews'] %}
                {% set keyTotal = series|reduce((carry, day) => carry + day[key], 0) %}
                <div class="analytics-stat" data-stat="{{ key }}">
                    <span class="analytics-stat-value">
                        <span class="analytics-stat-number" data-raw-value="{{ keyTotal }}">{{ keyTotal }}</span>
                        {%- if change[key] is not null %}
                            <span class="analytics-stat-change {{ change[key] >= 0 ? 'is-up' : 'is-down' }}">
                                {{ change[key] >= 0 ? '+' : '' }}{{ change[key] }}%
                            </span>
                        {% endif %}
                    </span>
                    <span class="analytics-stat-label">{{ palette[key].label }}</span>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
        {# Retention (Analytics::retention()): the share of the previous
           window's visitors and users who came back in this one. Beside the
           stats rather than on the chart - a ratio between two windows has no
           per-day value to plot. The range picker updates it with the rest
           (updateRetention() in layout.html.twig); hidden while there is no
           earlier window to measure against. #}
        <div class="analytics-retention" data-analytics-retention
             data-label-visitors="{{ 'analytics.retention.visitors'|trans({}, 'admin') }}"
             data-label-users="{{ 'analytics.retention.users'|trans({}, 'admin') }}"
             title="{{ 'analytics.retention.hint'|trans({}, 'admin') }}"
             {% if retention.visitors is null and retention.users is null %}hidden{% endif %}>
            <span class="analytics-retention-title">{{ 'analytics.retention.label'|trans({}, 'admin') }}</span>
            <span data-retention="visitors" {% if retention.visitors is null %}hidden{% endif %}>{{ 'analytics.retention.visitors'|trans({}, 'admin') }} {{ retention.visitors }}%</span>
            <span data-retention="users" {% if retention.users is null %}hidden{% endif %}>{{ 'analytics.retention.users'|trans({}, 'admin') }} {{ retention.users }}%</span>
        </div>
    </div>
    {# The range picker below now re-renders this stat row too (see
       layout.html.twig's own handler, keyed off each [data-stat]) instead
       of only ever swapping the chart underneath it - the two used to
       silently contradict each other the instant a different range got
       picked (chart showing 30 days of data next to a stat row still
       captioned with whatever window the page happened to load with). #}
    <div class="analytics-range-picker" data-analytics-range-picker data-analytics-endpoints="{{ {
        today: path('admin_analytics_breakdown', {range: 'today'}),
        '7d': path('admin_analytics_breakdown', {range: '7d'}),
        '14d': path('admin_analytics_breakdown', {range: '14d'}),
        '30d': path('admin_analytics_breakdown', {range: '30d'}),
        all: path('admin_analytics_breakdown', {range: 'all'}),
    }|json_encode }}">
        {% for range in ['today', '7d', '14d', '30d', 'all'] %}
            <button type="button" class="analytics-range-btn {{ range == '14d' ? 'is-active' }}" data-range="{{ range }}" {% if range == 'all' %}data-since-prefix="{{ 'analytics.range.since_prefix'|trans({}, 'admin') }}"{% endif %}>{{ ('analytics.range.' ~ range)|trans({}, 'admin') }}</button>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
    <div class="analytics-chart-wrap">
        {# palette|keys, not palette itself - Twig's map() preserves an
           associative source's own keys (verified: {a:.., b:..}|map(...)
           json_encodes as {"a":..,"b":..}, not [..,..]), and PHP's
           json_encode in turn renders ANY string-keyed array as a JS
           object, not an array. admin-charts.js's chartDatasets() calls
           .map() on config.datasets, which objects don't have - the
           whole chart silently failed to construct at all. palette|keys
           is a plain sequential list (0,1,2...) the same way
           article_views.html.twig's own hardcoded key list already is,
           so mapping over THAT keeps datasets a real JSON array. #}
        {# id, not just data-admin-chart: transparentJS carries existing
           canvases across a page swap so a chart is not redrawn, and it
           matches old to new BY ID. Without one it falls back to locating
           the equivalent parent in the incoming DOM, which fails inside
           the nest overlay - it then logs "Unexpected canvas without ID
           found" AND returns false, aborting the loop so every later
           canvas is skipped too. The widget key makes it stable across
           renders and unique per card. #}
        <canvas id="admin-chart-{{ widget.key|default('analytics')|replace({'.': '-'}) }}" data-admin-chart="{{ {
            labels: series|map(day => day.date|date('d/m')),
            datasets: palette|keys|map(key => {
                key: key,
                label: palette[key].label,
                data: series|map(day => day[key]),
                color: palette[key].color,
                colorDark: palette[key].colorDark,
                hidden: key not in visibleSeries,
            }),
            events: events|default([]),
        }|json_encode }}"></canvas>
    </div>
</div>
